From s.su at qut.edu.au  Mon Dec  2 05:25:47 2002
From: s.su at qut.edu.au (Steve Su)
Date: Mon, 02 Dec 2002 15:25:47 +1000
Subject: Summary: Transporting functions to different Splus platforms.
Message-ID: <000001c299c3$4876e8a0$4f32b583@busacc325>

Dear All,

Between different Windows Version of Splus, Madeline Bauer
[mbauer at usc.edu] suggested:

Have all of your functions in a splus working directory, 

then in S-Plus, use the data.dump (ls(), file = "steve.dat")

exit S-Plus, go to that subdirectory and copy the file steve.dat 

ON the new machine, set up the S-Plus project or chapter for your
functions, and use the command
data.restore(file="steve.dat").

However between UNIX and windows Version of Splus, Bill Dunlap
[bill at insightful.com] commented:

As of Splus 6 or so an ordinary .Data directory full of Splus binary
files made on any platform is readable on any other platform.

Unfortunately, the __Objects files made by the dumpChapter() function
followed by the "booting" step are not readable on machines of different
byte order or word size.  (The all.Sdata and meta.Sdata files made by
dumpChapter() may be read on any machine.)

We do not now have a good system for distributing compiled code for
various platforms.

I hope this information is useful for other users of Splus.

Steve.

