From robert.burbidge at csfb.com  Mon Dec  2 16:37:34 2002
From: robert.burbidge at csfb.com (Burbidge, Robert)
Date: Mon, 02 Dec 2002 16:37:34 +0000
Subject: Re: 1) Row identification in if statement, and 2) creating
Message-ID: <916B4FCD8EDAD1119F7800A0C98F17BA0CCF2895@slon12302.csfp.co.uk>

> You can use cascading calls to ifelse; if the entries of 
> TruncA can only be 
> 1, 2, or 3, then something like the following should work:
> 
>          ifelse(TruncA == 1, whatever happens for ones,
>            ifelse(TruncA ==2, whatever happens for twos,
>              whatever happens for threes))
> 
> The three "whatever happens" expressions must produce vectors 
> of the same 
> length as TruncA. See ?ifelse for details.

Cindy,

I note that you had:

truncA=1 # assignment

instead of 

truncA==1 # equality 

Instead of ifelse() you could use switch(), if truncA is a scalar:

ystar <- switch(truncA, 

        test211$Fc1*TruncB+test211$Fc2*ySurvM+test211$Fc3*ySurvM^2+
        test211$Fc4*ySurvM^3+test211$Fc5*ySurvM^4+test211$Fc6*ySurvM^5,

        test211$Fc2*ySurvM*TruncB+test211$Fc3*ySurvM^2+test211$Fc4*ySurvM^3+
        test211$Fc5*ySurvM^4+test211$Fc6*ySurvM^5,

        test211$Fc3*TruncB*ySurvM^2+test211$Fc4*ySurvM^3+test211$Fc5*ySurvM^4+
        test211$Fc6*ySurvM^5)

The value of truncA (1, 2, or 3) specifies the position in the list of the 
return value.

If truncA is a vector, then use sapply()

ystar <- sapply(truncA, switch, 

        test211$Fc1*TruncB+test211$Fc2*ySurvM+test211$Fc3*ySurvM^2+
        test211$Fc4*ySurvM^3+test211$Fc5*ySurvM^4+test211$Fc6*ySurvM^5,

        test211$Fc2*ySurvM*TruncB+test211$Fc3*ySurvM^2+test211$Fc4*ySurvM^3+
        test211$Fc5*ySurvM^4+test211$Fc6*ySurvM^5,

        test211$Fc3*TruncB*ySurvM^2+test211$Fc4*ySurvM^3+test211$Fc5*ySurvM^4+
        test211$Fc6*ySurvM^5)

Rgds,

Robert

