From scott.chasalow at monsanto.com  Thu Dec  5 01:50:38 2002
From: scott.chasalow at monsanto.com (CHASALOW, SCOTT [AG/1000])
Date: Wed, 04 Dec 2002 19:50:38 -0600
Subject: Re: Efficiency Question
Message-ID: <9548328D5FFCD411BB7A00508BAFB94D08C6E093@ems1000-07.monsanto.com>

I think you will find the following rather more efficient; probably more
stable numerically also.

qqq <- log(1 - p)
ppp <- log(p)
g <- exp(X %*% t(ppp) + (1 - X) %*% t(qqq))

Here, we have taken logs to change products into sums.  This allows us to
avoid multiplying many possibly small numbers together.  And, we can use the
relatively efficient matrix multiplication to pick out the required elements
of p or 1 - p, and do the sums.

You will need to take some care if any elements of p are 0 or 1 (so that
log(p) or log(1 - p) is -Inf).  The main difficulty I have found with this
sort of thing is that 0 * (-Inf) evaluates to NA.  That's reasonable enough
on its own, I suppose.  But in your application, multiplication by 0 is just
a simple trick to leave the term out of the sum.  You want 0 * (-Inf) to be
0 here.

Here's one possible, if inelegant, solution to this complication: change any
-Inf values in ppp or qqq to a very large (in absolute value) but finite
negative number.

qqq <- log(1 - p)
ppp <- log(p)
ppp[is.infinite(ppp)] <- (-.Machine$double.xmax)
qqq[is.infinite(qqq)] <- (-.Machine$double.xmax)
g <- exp(X %*% t(ppp) + (1 - X) %*% t(qqq))

Another possibility (less efficient, more memory intensive, but avoids the 0
* -Inf problem more directly):

nrx <- nrow(X)
nrp <- nrow(p)
Xnow <- as.vector(t(X[rep(1:nrx, rep(nrp, nrx)), , drop = F]))
pnow <- as.vector(t(p))
g <- log(pnow^Xnow) + log((1 - pnow)^(1 - Xnow))
g <- exp(colSums(g))
dim(g) <- c(nrp, nrx)
g <- t(g)

I haven't tested this last bit of code, but it, or something quite like it,
should work.

Cheers,
Scott

