It's not possible automatically AFAIK, but see the relevant sections in
V&R's `S Programming' and the resources() function there (which owes much
to Bill Dunlap).

In particular

sys.time(a<- blah blah)

tells you how long that command took (in S+6; see the reference for
earlier versions of S-PLUS).

It is possible to profile in R, and I have several times got my students
to run the code in R and profile it to see where the bottlenecks are.
They need of course not be in the same places as in S-PLUS, but each time
it has been a very helpful exercise.

Profiling tells you in a run of a block of code how much time was spent in
each function.

