Dear S-Plus users,
I realy am a S-plus die-hard, but on my iMac at home I rather work with R.
Nearly all the functions in S-Plus work with R, except gam(), which is a
completely different implementation (and maybe more useful at that)  and
special functions like rowSums and the like (which are not needed anyway
when using apply). Another sometimes annoying thing is the use of the
argument 'where' (in objects(..,where=1), R uses pos=-1 and with a
different meaning. Of course there are other differences (brian Ripley and
Bill Venables have communicated this several times) but for simple
practical purposes one can live with that. R is fast, perhaps also because
there is no GUI, it looks like S (-Plus) in the old days on Unix.
Siem Heisterkamp

From jimsailgr at yahoo.gr  Fri Dec  6 13:23:55 2002
From: jimsailgr at yahoo.gr (Dimitris Rizopoulos)
Date: Fri, 06 Dec 2002 13:23:55 +0000
Subject: Summary: Efficiency Question
Message-ID: <20021206132355.83348.qmail@web40811.mail.yahoo.com>

I would like to thank Bill Dunlap, Scott D. Chasalow and Tim
Hesterberg for their useful suggestions to my inquiry.

Bill Dunlap's suggestion:
g <- matrix(1.0, nrow=nrow(X), ncol=nrow(p))
for(k in 1:ncol(X)){
g <- g*outer(X[,k], p[,k], function(x,p) ifelse(x==1,p,1-p))
}

Scott Chasalow's suggestion:
qqq <- log(1-p)
ppp <- log(p)
g <- exp(X%*%t(ppp)+(1-X)%*%t(qqq))

Tim Hesterberg's suggestion:
qqq <- log(1-p)
ppp <- log(p)
g <- exp(rowSums(X*log(p)+(1-X)*log(1-p)))

Thanks again,
Dimitris

