Subject: Splus for MacOS X
Message-ID: <CFA6739DD532764AA51E7459970BF0E205D0A99A@ns-bco-mse3.im.battelle.org>

Having read the earlier thread about using Splus on the new MacOS X,
I asked some of the folks at my local Linux User's Group about it.  The
concensus is that you have three reasonable solutions:

1.  Use VirtualPC and install your favorite version of Splus
on your computer.  The newest version of VirtualPC is availabe for about
$250US and (based on the descriptions off of the website) appears to
support both Linux, DOS, and Windows.  Go to 
http://www.connectix.com/products/vpc5m.html for more information.

2.  Make your Macintosh dual-boot.  Since the new MacOS X is based
off of Linux/BSD, you can easily add another Linux flavor and choose
to boot from whatever OS you like at startup.  Yellowdog Linux is the
flavor of choice to use with Apple hardware and several people replied
by saying that it is a good, stable Linux implementation.  Then, install
Splus for UNIX/Linux in your Yellowdog OS and away you go.  This
solution will result in a faster implementation than VirtualPC since you
won't be going through an interpreter.

3.  Spend about $200 at Wal-Mart (wal-mart.com) to get a cheap
Wintel box, download your favorite Windows OS, and forget about
using Splus on a Macintosh.

