Hi, every one on the list, Season Greetings.

 

I have question on Type I and III sum of squares given by analysis of variance.

Say I have three factors in my ANOVA model, the anova function can calculate Type I and III sum of squares for each of the three factors in the model. As indicated in splus menu, Type I sum of squares for a factor in the model is depended on the factor's order in the model formula. The sum of Type I sums of squares of all factors is equal to the total sum of squares (sum(y_i - y_-)^2). The Type III sum of squares for a factor is said not depending on its order in the model formula, and yet the sum of the sums of squares of all factors is not the same as the total sum of squares. The difference between sum of squares of Type I and that of Type III is not very significant in my study. However I wonder if any splus function can give me the sum of squares for each of  my three variables and these sums of squares can satisfy: (a) they are additive, in the sense that they are added up to the total sum of squares;  (b) the sum of squares for a factor in the model is not associated with the factor's order in the model formula. 

 

Thank you very much for your kind help.

 

Jo

