Subject: Re: Type I and III sum of squares
Message-ID: <51F9C42DA15CD311BD220008C707D81906FFC9A4@usrymx10.merck.com>

What 
you're asking for is mathematically impossible for data with non-orthogonal 
design matrix (as is the case with "unbalanced" design: unequal cell counts or 
missing cells).  Also, asking for "nice properties" of sum of squares is 
really barking up the wrong tree.  Those mathematical niceties serve no 
real useful purpose in answering real data analysis questions.  (So 
what if the SS are additive and order-independent?  What does that have 
to do with your scientific questions?)

 

As Bill 
Venables said, the right question to ask is what are the hypotheses you're 
interested in testing?  If you can write that down clear enough, there 
won't be silly questions like which is the right type of SS to 
use.

 

Amen to 
what Bill said!

 

Andy

 

