Subject: Re: Mc Nemar Test
Message-ID: <3DF89E23.6FCC5347@mcgill.ca>

Hello, In contingency table analysis one reduces the degrees of freedom
of a Chi-Sq by the presence of 'structural zeros' i.e. positions in the
table that 'by design' are impossible to occur. If a cell is zero due to
sampling variation, it is a data point equal zero and no df should be
subtracted.
In the McNemar test is the same, if 2 corresponding cells (1,3) and
(3,1) in the example are both zero, that is perfect agreement! no
subtraction of df. A better computerized solution would be to add
epsilon=0.0000001 to each denominator, the final Chi-sq will be
minimally affected or use denoms[denoms==0]<-1 if the denom is zero so
is the numerator and the contribution to the total Chi2 will be zero.
Just my 2 CDN(Quebec) cents

