From andrew.cooper at unh.edu  Fri Dec 13 16:15:30 2002
From: andrew.cooper at unh.edu (Andrew Cooper)
Date: Fri, 13 Dec 2002 11:15:30 -0500
Subject: denDF for lme - Splus vs SAS
In-Reply-To: <002801c2a1cd$86ada610$0a434ea0@marcoriani>
References: <002801c2a1cd$86ada610$0a434ea0@marcoriani>
Message-ID: <Pine.OSF.4.44.0212131106030.371830-100000@alberti.unh.edu>

Hello s-newsers,

I'm trying to help a colleague analyze some data.  He uses proc mixed (SAS
v8.02 ) and I use lme() (Splus (2000 Professional) - both on Windows 2000
platforms, and we can't seem to get SAS and Splus to calculate the
demoniator degrees of freedom in the same way for a pretty
straight-forward mixed-effects model.

At each of six different locations, my colleague placed 12 cages
containing a fixed number of clams.  6 of these cages were completely
closed, 6 had their tops removed to allow access to predators.  We have an
estimate for the abundance of crabs resident at each site (constant for
all cages within a site).  Our dependent variable is percent of clams
in a cage that died (for now we're transforming the percentage and
assuming normality, later we'll move to a binomial distribution).
So, we have 12 observations at each site (6 from cages with tops, 6
from cages without tops) and a total of 6 sites.  There is no missing
data.

Our model, for now, takes the form of:

Percent_mort = intercept + crab_abund + Top_on

Where crab_abund is a continuous variable for the abundance of crabs
(constant for all cages within a site, different between sites) and Top_on
is a categorical variable (cage has a top, cage does not have a top).

When we have only a random intercept (allowing percent_mort to differ by
site), proc mixed and lme() give us the same answers (see below).  When we
allow Top_on to differ by site along with a random intercept (having a
random effect for Top_on as well as a fixed effect), Splus and SAS
calculate the denDF very differently. In Splus, the denDF for the fixed
effect of Top_on does not change when we add the random effect of Top_on.
In SAS the denDF goes from 65 down to 5 (see below).

I'm hoping that someone on this list might be able to explain to me if I'm
doing something wrong in Splus (and the denDF should change), if he's
doing something wrong in SAS (the denDF should not change), or if the two
programs are simply based on different approaches (and if so, which is
more appropriate).

Thank you for your help!

Cheers,
Andy

Splus:

silly1 <- lme(fixed = precent_mort ~ crab_abund + Top_on,random =
~1|Site,data = mydata)
avova(silly1)

            numDF denDF  F-value p-value
(Intercept)     1    65 130.7473  <.0001
     Top_on     1    65  48.8782  <.0001
 Crab_abund     1     4   9.0125  0.0399

SAS:

proc mixed data = mydata;
class site pred;
model percent_mort = crab_abund Top_on/ s ddfm = satterth;
random  int / subject = site;
run;

Type 3 Tests of Fixed Effects

Effect        numDF  denDF   F Value    Pr > F
Top_on         1      65      48.88    <.0001
crab_abund     1       4       9.01    0.0399

However, when we allow Top_on to vary by site, Splus and SAS give
different answers:

Splus:

silly2 <- lme(fixed = precent_mort ~ crab_abund + Top_on,random =
~Top_on|Site,data = mydata)
avova(silly2)

            numDF denDF  F-value p-value
(Intercept)     1    65 146.1387  <.0001
     Top_on     1    65  14.5841  0.0003
 Crab_abund     1     4   6.1506  0.0682

SAS:

proc mixed data = mydata;
class site pred;
model percent_mort = crab_abund Top_on/ s ddfm = satterth;
random  int Top_on / subject = site;
run;

Type 3 Tests of Fixed Effects

Effect            numDF  denDF   F Value    Pr > F
Top_on             1       5      15.40    0.0111
Crab_abund         1       4       9.01    0.0399
(note: the same thing happens when ddfm = kenwardroger)

For some reason, when we allow Top_on to vary by site, SAS changes the
degrees of freedom for the fixed effect to equal 5 while Splus keeps it at
65.  Does anyone have an idea of why there's a difference?

From datkins at u.washington.edu  Fri Dec 13 16:25:40 2002
From: datkins at u.washington.edu (Dave Atkins)
Date: Fri, 13 Dec 2002 08:25:40 -0800
Subject: Re: denDF for lme - Splus vs SAS
References: <Pine.OSF.4.44.0212131106030.371830-100000@alberti.unh.edu>
Message-ID: <005501c2a2c4$47214e50$f2c48e8c@washington.edu>

Andy--

If you have access to Pinheiro & Bates' book, they describe how the dfs are
calculated (I believe at the end of chapter 2 -- don't have it with me at
present).  However, a while back there was a discussion of this issue on the
MULTILEVEL listserv.  My understanding is that there are some genuine
disagreements regarding the correct way to calculate dfs in mixed effects
models (or at least various plausible ways to do it).  I personally don't
use SAS but have run into a similar issue between the HLM program and lme;
thus, it's not specific to those two programs.  If you search the archives
of the MULTILEVEL listserv, you should be able to track down the discussion.
I think you should be able to find the archives at:

http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/multilevel.html

This is just my two cents.  I'd be interested for others opinions as well.

cheers, Dave

Dave Atkins
University of Washington
