Dear All,

Thank you all of you: Nick Ellis, Patrick Burns, Gabriel Baud Bovy, and
Tim Hesterberg who replied to me in my aggregate question. 

I have a few interesting solutions:

Nick Ellis recommendation is a modification of the agg.sum function to
account for matrices, however he also noted there is a rounding error
issue there. 

agg.sum.mult <- 
function(x, by, sort.it = F)
{
        if(!is.data.frame(by))
                by <- data.frame(by)
        if(sort.it) {
                ord <- do.call("order", unname(by))
                x <- x[ord,]
                by <- by[ord,  ]
        }
        logical.diff <- function(group)
        group[-1] != group[ - length(group)]
        change <- logical.diff(by[[1]])
        for(i in seq(along = by)[-1])
                change <- change | logical.diff(by[[i]])
        by <- by[c(T, change),  , drop = F]
        #browser()
        xsum <- diff(rbind(0, apply(x,2,cumsum))[c(T, change),])
        dimnames(xsum)[[2]] <- dimnames(x)[[2]]
        by <- cbind(by,xsum)
        by
}

Gabriel Baud Bovy recommended the following solution:

index<-split(1:nrow(mat),paste(rep(1:3,each=3),rep(1:2,c(2,7))))
        sapply(index,function(i,mat)colSums(mat[i,,drop=F]),mat)

Tim Hesterberg commented there is a resample library in Splus
(pre-released beta version) which has a groupSums function that will do
the trick: 

aggregate(j,list(rep(1:3,each=3),rep(1:2,c(2,7))),colSums)
        groupSums(j, paste(rep(1:3,each=3),rep(1:2,c(2,7))))

Patrick Burns suggested me to look at writing a C code.

I propose that perhaps, a somewhat faster alternative to aggregate is
the following function: 

fun.aggregate.mat<-function(x,index,fun){

agg.ind<-do.call('paste',index)

tt<-cbind(do.call('cbind',index),agg.ind)
tt<-tt[!duplicated(tt[, ncol(tt)]),  ][,1:length(index)]

result<-data.frame(tt,t(sapply(split(1:nrow(x),agg.ind),function(i,x,fun
)fun(x[i,,drop=F]),x,fun)))[unique(agg.ind),]

return(result)

}

And fun.aggregate.mat(j,list(rep(1:3,each=3),rep(1:2,c(2,7))),colSums)
will do the trick. This solution follows directly from Gabriel Baud
Bovy. I have not test this extensively so there might be errors in the
above function. Those who can believe my function can be modified to
improve the accuracy or speed, feel free to comment and criticise!

