Pete, I certainly agree, and the test does have to be suitable for
publication. The real data I'm concerned with is subjective classification
of paired hot and freeze brand healing response variables (two observations
on each animal). 
 
When I compared the Stuart-Maxwell and McNemar tests, I realized that they
only differed with respect to the number of degrees of freedom used to
assign p values to the chi-square (for the example, perhaps they differ in
other ways for different scenarios). Hence I expect you are right about the
S+ version of McNemar applying 2 X 2 logic regardless of k. 
 
I also expect you are right about the weakness of a chi-square for these
data. But I believe the chi-square will only fail in terms of attributing
insignificance where a more appropriate resolution would determine a
significant difference (agree?). In our situation, we have a very large
number of variables (10-12) over a number of conditioners (4 skin layers),
so I'm not greatly concerned about losing a proportion of significant
results. We also have half a dozen non-subjective continuous variates in the
mix. These are not problematic to test, and provide a reality check on the
categorical responses. 
 
Of course I am still seeking a more appropriate test. At present I am asking
some colleagues to pass my example through SAS and SPSS, and also to try the
marginal homogeneity test. I may hear back re SAS fairly soon if the
near-Xmas timing doesn't work against me. I have not yet succeeded in
finding anyone with the Exact Tests module in SPSS. Once I have these
comparitive results I hope to obtain a better idea of the direction to take.
 
It does not appear very likely that the S-news list will have any more to
say on this subject. If I ultimately affect a resolution using S+ I will
summarize details to the list. But if I have to use another package, would
you (or anyone else) still like a summary?

-----Original Message-----
From: Pete Meyer [mailto:pmeyer160 at comcast.net]
Sent: Friday, December 13, 2002 6:59 PM
To: Fowler, Mark
Subject: One more thought on McNemar...

You know, Mark, I am still a little troubled about using McNemar in
this situation.  If it was a 2x2, it's clear that you would want an 
exact binomial p-value, rather than the chi-square, since the sample
size you have is not adequate for the chi-square to be appropriate.
 
I think the kxk McNemar's that Splus has implemented is just summing
them as if they were independent chi-squares.  With the small counts
that you have they probably aren't chi-square and with ordinal outcomes, 
they probably aren't independent, either.
 
I've used weighted kappa statistics to describe agreement, which will work
with some ordered variables.  Differences in category assignment
are weighted according to their differences on the ordinal scale.
However, for values of kappa near 0 or 1 these have their problems as well.
 
I don't know whether this is being used as a sieve, to identify 
potential predictors from a large set of possible ones, or whether
this has to hold muster for publication.  For the former, ad hoc
things tend to be useful, even though not theoretically accurate.
For the latter, I think I would want to think carefully about what
the question is and then look for a form of analysis that deals with
the small cell counts you have appropriately.
 
Pete
