 I had previously modified the mcnemar.test to eliminate cases of zero
denominators and decrement the associated degrees of freedom. Heberto Ghezzo
advises that the degrees of freedom should not be decremented in situations
where a cell could be expected to be non-zero at larger sample sizes:

Hello, In contingency table analysis one reduces the degrees of freedom

of a Chi-Sq by the presence of 'structural zeros' i.e. positions in the

table that 'by design' are impossible to occur. If a cell is zero due to

sampling variation, it is a data point equal zero and no df should be

subtracted.

In the McNemar test is the same, if 2 corresponding cells (1,3) and

(3,1) in the example are both zero, that is perfect agreement! no

subtraction of df. A better computerized solution would be to add

epsilon=0.0000001 to each denominator, the final Chi-sq will be

minimally affected or use denoms[denoms==0]<-1 if the denom is zero so

is the numerator and the contribution to the total Chi2 will be zero.

Just my 2 CDN(Quebec) cents

This almost corresponds to the way degrees of freedom are handled in a
Stuart-Maxwell test. The only difference for a 3 X 3 that I can see is
taking the Chi-square at one less degree of freedom with Stuart-Maxwell,
which may be appropriate for this example (this difference between tests
holds for equal-levels situations). But here I just leave the degrees of
freedom alone as recommended. Editing the test to replace 0-denoms with
1's...

#Revision M Fowler Dec 12 2002 (as per Ghezzo)

#Replacing 0's with 1 for all denominators

#This gives the same result as removing the 0-denoms/nums or 

# adding a small value (0.0000001) to all denoms but leaving df alone

#Leaving the degrees of freedom as is

denoms[denoms == 0] <- 1

#End of revision

produces (same data as earlier example below)...

> mcnx.test(table(x,y))

McNemar's chi-square test without continuity correction

data: table(x, y) 

McNemar's chi-square = 3, df = 3, p-value = 0.3916 

The p-value here for 3 df seems more believable for this example than the
.0833 we got for 1 df. But we don't have much in the way of confidence that
we're resolving the issue appropriately. For now I'll just flag
unequal-levels test results as dubious. It might be more helpful generally
to address the way mcnemar.test ascribes degrees of freedom beyond 2 X 2
data, and the solution to the unequal-levels problem may be a result.

