From kdiet at u.washington.edu  Mon Dec 16 22:37:12 2002
From: kdiet at u.washington.edu (Kimberly S Dietrich)
Date: Mon, 16 Dec 2002 14:37:12 -0800
Subject: specifying dispersion param & model compare quest
Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.43.0212161009130.18587-100000@hymn08.u.washington.edu>

Hello-

I have several GLM & GAM models and would like advice on which method to
use to find the 'best' fit and how to specify a constant dispersion
parameter so I'm comparing apples to apples.  There are a couple of
complicating issues I should lay out first.  My dependent variable is
composed of count data which are  ~90% zeros.   I'm using
family=quasi(link=log, var=mu) so that  the dispersion parameter is
calculated from the data rather than an  assumed constant. I've included
an offset() variable to account for differences in size of my sampling
units. The main objective of my work is not to have the best predictive
model, I would rather know which variables have the most influence on the
count (seabird bycatch in my case) and are also significant. I'm using
SPlus 2000.

To my (limited) knowledge, Methods to find 'best' fit include (I'll try
to keep this as short as I can):
1. Use step.glm() or step.gam() to allow forward & backward selection.
Appears to use Cp statistic to include & exclude variables.  Output also
lists "AIC:" but this number appears to really just be the residual
deviance. Disadvantage  of this method is one cannot specify the order
variables are loaded.

2. Use drop1() or drop1.lm() for models created using glm().  If any Cp is
less than the <none> Cp value, remove variable w/ lowest Cp and re-run.
Repeat until all Cp's are greater than <none> Cp. (SPlus User Guide, vol
1, p.179-181)

3. Use add1() or add1.lm() for models created using glm(). Starts w/ null
model & makes single term additions. Calculates an anova (test=Cp) for
each addition against the previous model.  If Cp increases from previous,
variable is not important. (SPlus User Guide, p 304)

4. start w/ all variables in model. Drop individually. Use AIC(model 1,
model 2, etc) to select model w/ lowest AIC.  Repeat until cannot delete
any variables.

So, I think I have 2 at least problems.  First, I can use the scale=
argument within step.glm()/step.gam()/add1.lm() to set the dispersion
parameter to a constant(Chambers & Hastie 1992).  How do I select what
this constant should be?  For example, the range of the disp.param. for a
series of models w/ different variables dropped is between 3.05 to 3.35.
Can I just base it on previous output of the model w/ all variables?

Second, when comparing glm objects with gam objects is it appropriate to
use the anova(glm.object, gam.object, test="Cp")? Do both models need to
have identical variables?

Any input on recommended model selection methods and the 2 issues above
would be greatly appreciated.  Thank you.

Kim Dietrich
Graduate student
University of Washington
School of Aquatic & Fishery Sciences
P.O. Box 355020
Seattle, WA 98195  USA

