From therneau at mayo.edu  Mon Dec 30 13:14:10 2002
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Terry Therneau)
Date: Mon, 30 Dec 2002 07:14:10 -0600
Subject: Re: extracting pixel values from a TIF in S+
Message-ID: <200212301314.gBUDEAC02550@rocky.mayo.edu>

  I have had success reading TIFF images using the libtiff routines
(www.libtiff.org).  My problem was actually somewhat harder: I was reading
"psuedo-tiff" files as produced by HP and Molecular Dynamics imagers in the
biochemistry labs; these are usually used to image Western blots.  The tiff
format allows for application specific tags, e.g., extra information, to be
added to the file, and one of these tags was needed by my analysis.  In order
to store a wide dynamic range of values in a small file, the scanners actually
write 16 bit integer files with value = round(sqrt(actual value * scale)) --
and the scale was one of the "extra" tags.  (My final analysis didn't actually 
need the scale, but I got tired of all the invalid tag warning messages each
time I read a file).

  First, download and compile the library.  If the supplied "readtiff" and
"dumptiff" routines can make sense of your files, then you are in business.
I can supply a copy of my S and C code.  The extra tags were a pain, but other-
wise it's pretty simple.  

  This was all done on Unix and Linux.  You mileage may vary with inferior
operating systems....

        Terry Therneau

